hello I'm Andy hunt from Wolfram  research and I'm here to talk about a  new feature in Mathematica 11.3 called  presenter tools that Mathematica has had  the ability to present notebooks and  slideshows for many releases but now  it's version 11.3 we've added an  integrator toolkit that provides  additional functionality to help you  author and present your notebooks to the  world to create a new presentation  choose file new presenter notebook from  the menus this brings up a dialog from  which you can choose from several custom  themes along with the optional ability  to change the font sets you can pre-fill  your presentation with sample content or  start off with an empty notebook  presenter tools as an integrated tool  bar which provides quick access to  commonly used features inserting new  slides inserting new styled cell  templates and various formatting buttons  to help you modify cell content  there are also buttons to increase and  decrease cell margins to position your  content around the screen once you've  modified the cell formatting you can  apply those changes back to the style  sheet choosing which options to apply  from the dialog this will also update  similar Styles in your notebook and all  future instances of the same style  there's also a companion formatting  palette available from the toolbar menu  also in the menu is a dialog where you  can change keyboard shortcuts used when  navigating your presentation to divide  existing content into individual slides  you can add a new slide break as I did  before or you can set your default slide  breaks based on cell styles a slight  break cell will be added above each cell  style YouTube  the cell styles you choose will also be  added to the toolbars cell style menu if  you've added content by hand instead of  going through the menus again you can  also click refresh the slide breaks in  your notebook  presentation notebooks are responsive so  increasing and decreasing the notebook  window size will scale the content  accordingly by default graphics will  scale on your presentation  but you can choose whether or not the  graphics are responsive through the  toolbar menu the window size menu in the  toolbar contains several common the used  settings if you're creating content to  fit a specific monitor or projectors  resolution to see an overview of your  presentation clicking on the outline  palette will provide a thumbnail view of  your presentation along with clickable  icons when you're ready to present your  notebook  click start presentation presenter tools  provides a menu in the upper right  corner which contains navigation  controls as well as toggling a  navigation bar access to the style  palette and access to the presentation  controls dialog choosing in presentation  takes you back to working view the view  with the toolbar one helpful feature of  presenter tools are side note these are  special cells are visible in the  authoring view but are hidden from view  during your presentation they can be  accessed from the bottom of the cell  style menu  a pallet collating these notes can be  accessed from the toolbar and during  your presentation side notes can also be  accessed from the menu for side notes  code you also have the ability to paste  code from the pallet into your notebook  and if you right click it will paste and  evaluate these are just some of the many  features of presenter tools I appreciate  your time and look forward to your  comments and experiences using preserve  tools thank you
 

